SUMMARY:21JPSI Speaker Series "The Future of Space Security in Asia" (Pro
 f. Saadia Pekkanen (University of Washington))
DESCRIPTION:A public talk/Q&amp\;A about space security in Asia by one of
  the world's leading experts. Hodge Hall (HH) 2079&#160\;(corner of E 10t
 h St and N Fee Lane)  This public event is open to all students\, faculty
 \, staff\, and members of the community.Co-sponsored by the IU Institute 
 for Korean Studies and Space Governance Lab. Please join us!Space has eme
 rged as a consequential arena of strategic competition\, defense\, and co
 nflict. Asia\, with the greatest concentration of the world's top indepen
 dent space powers\, is at the forefront of these dynamics. Japan's growin
 g capabilities\, changing operational postures\, and deepening space coll
 aboration with the United States are contributing to – and helping shap
 e – this evolving strategic environment\, and broader prospects for war
  and peace.This event is co-sponsored by IU's Cybersecurity and Global Po
 licy Program\, the Department of Political Science\, and the Space Govern
 ance Lab.  ————————————Saadia M. Pekkanen is the 
 Job and Gertrud Tamaki Endowed Professor of International Studies\, Adjun
 ct Professor of Political Science\, and Adjunct Professor of Law at the U
 niversity of Washington in Seattle. She is founding director of the Progr
 am on Strategy\, Policy\, and Diplomacy Research (SPDR) at The Henry M. J
 ackson School of International Studies and the Founding Director of the S
 pace Law\, Data\, and Policy Program (SPACE LDP) at the School of Law. He
 r research interests are in outer space security\, diplomacy\, and intern
 ational affairs\, with a focus on Japan and Asia. She has published widel
 y\, and is most recently co-editor of the special issue on Space Diplomac
 y: The Final Frontier of Theory and Practice\, and co-editor of The Oxfor
 d Handbook of Space Security. She earned Master's degrees from Columbia U
 niversity and Yale Law School\, and a doctorate from Harvard University i
 n political science. She is an elected member of the International Instit
 ute of Space Law (IISL) and a lifetime member of the Council of Foreign R
 elations (CFR). For further information please visit\, saadiapekkanen.com
 .Adam LIFF (host/moderator) is Professor of East Asian International Rela
 tions at Indiana University's Hamilton Lugar School of Global and Interna
 tional Studies\, where he also serves as Founding Director of its 21st Ce
 ntury Japan Politics and Society Initiative ("21JPSI"). Beyond IU\, he is
  a Nonresident Senior Fellow with the Center for Asia Policy Studies at t
 he Brookings Institution\, as well as a Faculty Associate and Associate-i
 n-Research at Harvard University's Program on U.S.-Japan Relations and Re
 ischauer Institute of Japanese Studies.&#160\;His research examines conte
 mporary international security and foreign policy challenges in East Asia
 —with a particular focus on Japanese politics and foreign policy\; the 
 U.S.-Japan security alliance\; U.S. allies and Taiwan\; U.S. Asia-Pacific
  strategy\; and U.S.-Japan-China relations. He holds a Ph.D. and M.A. in 
 Politics from Princeton University\, a postgraduate research certificate 
 in International Politics from the University of Tokyo\, and a B.A. in Ea
 st Asian Studies and Psychology from Stanford University&#160\;All 21JPSI
  events are 100% in-person unless otherwise noted. This event is made pos
 sible thanks to generous support from the Japan Foundation.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<div id="lw_cal_event_detail_intro" class="l
 w_cal_event_detail_block"><p>    A public talk/Q&amp\;A about space secur
 ity in Asia by one of the world's leading experts. <strong>Hodge Hall (HH
 ) 2079&#160\;</strong>(corner of E 10th St and N Fee Lane)  </p></div><di
 v id="lw_cal_event_detail_description" class="lw_cal_event_detail_block">
 <p><em>This public event is open to all students\, faculty\, staff\, and 
 members of the community.</em><em>Co-sponsored by the IU Institute for Ko
 rean Studies and Space Governance Lab. Please join us!</em></p><p><span d
 ata-olk-copy-source="MailCompose">Space has emerged as a consequential ar
 ena of strategic competition\, defense\, and conflict. Asia\, with the gr
 eatest concentration of the world's top independent space powers\, is at 
 the forefront of these dynamics. Japan's growing capabilities\, changing 
 operational postures\, and deepening space collaboration with the United 
 States are contributing to – and helping shape – this evolving strate
 gic environment\, and broader prospects for war and peace.</span></p><p><
 span data-olk-copy-source="MailCompose"><em>This event is co-sponsored by
  IU's Cybersecurity and Global Policy Program\, the Department of Politic
 al Science\, and the Space Governance Lab.</em></span></p></div><p>  —�
 ��——————————</p><div class="profile-about"><div itemp
 rop="description articleSection"><p><span data-olk-copy-source="MailCompo
 se"><strong>Saadia M. Pekkanen</strong> is the Job and Gertrud Tamaki End
 owed Professor of International Studies\, Adjunct Professor of Political 
 Science\, and Adjunct Professor of Law at the University of Washington in
  Seattle. She is founding director of the Program on Strategy\, Policy\, 
 and Diplomacy Research (SPDR) at The Henry M. Jackson School of Internati
 onal Studies and the Founding Director of the Space Law\, Data\, and Poli
 cy Program (SPACE LDP) at the School of Law. Her research interests are i
 n outer space security\, diplomacy\, and international affairs\, with a f
 ocus on Japan and Asia. She has published widely\, and is most recently c
 o-editor of the special issue on <i>Space Diplomacy: The Final Frontier o
 f Theory and Practice</i>\, and co-editor of <i>The Oxford Handbook of Sp
 ace Security</i>. She earned Master's degrees from Columbia University an
 d Yale Law School\, and a doctorate from Harvard University in political 
 science. She is an elected member of the International Institute of Space
  Law (IISL) and a lifetime member of the Council of Foreign Relations (CF
 R). For further information please visit\, saadiapekkanen.com.</span></p>
 </div></div><p><strong>Adam LIFF (host/moderator)</strong> is Professor o
 f East Asian International Relations at Indiana University's Hamilton Lug
 ar School of Global and International Studies\, where he also serves as F
 ounding Director of its 21st Century Japan Politics and Society Initiativ
 e ("21JPSI"). Beyond IU\, he is a Nonresident Senior Fellow with the Cent
 er for Asia Policy Studies at the Brookings Institution\, as well as a Fa
 culty Associate and Associate-in-Research at Harvard University's Program
  on U.S.-Japan Relations and Reischauer Institute of Japanese Studies.&#1
 60\;His research examines contemporary international security and foreign
  policy challenges in East Asia—with a particular focus on Japanese pol
 itics and foreign policy\; the U.S.-Japan security alliance\; U.S. allies
  and Taiwan\; U.S. Asia-Pacific strategy\; and U.S.-Japan-China relations
 . He holds a Ph.D. and M.A. in Politics from Princeton University\, a pos
 tgraduate research certificate in International Politics from the Univers
 ity of Tokyo\, and a B.A. in East Asian Studies and Psychology from Stanf
 ord University&#160\;</p><p><em>All 21JPSI events are 100% in-person unle
 ss otherwise noted. This event is made possible thanks to generous suppor
 t from the Japan Foundation.</em></p>
